['How did the telegraph change long-distance communication?','It allowed coded messages to travel electrically','It carried messages only by sailing boat|It showed live colour television|It required every sender to travel with the message','Telegraphs could transmit messages much faster than physical delivery over long distances.',2,null,'communication'],
['Why did reliable refrigeration change food storage?','Cooling slowed spoilage and helped preserve many foods','It made every food last forever|It removed all need for hygiene|It turned food into medicine','Refrigeration helped households and businesses store and transport food. Safe handling remained important.',1,null,'daily-life'],
['What major exploration event did Apollo 11 achieve in 1969?','The first human landing on the Moon','The first landing on Mars|The invention of writing|The first journey to another galaxy','Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon; Michael Collins remained in lunar orbit. The mission relied on a large team.',1,'moon','major-events'],
['Why should the Moon landing story include engineers and mathematicians as well as astronauts?','Large achievements depend on many people’s contributions','Only astronauts ever worked on the mission|Maths was unnecessary|Spacecraft build themselves','Planning, calculations, construction and mission support all contributed to Apollo 11.',2,'moon','history-people'],
['What did the World Wide Web make easier for many computer users?','Accessing and linking pages of information over the internet','Sending paper letters without stamps|Travelling physically through space|Generating electricity from screens','The Web is a system of linked resources using the internet. The Web and the internet are related but not identical.',2,null,'communication'],
['How can an old photograph help someone investigate school life in the past?','It can show visible details such as clothing, buildings and equipment','It reveals every pupil’s thoughts|It proves every school was identical|It always shows the whole day','Photographs are evidence, but their date, purpose and what lies outside the frame matter.',2,null,'historical-evidence'],
['What does an oral history preserve?','A person’s spoken memories and experiences','Only numbers printed on coins|A complete recording of all past events|A fossil’s exact age','Oral histories preserve experiences and perspectives. Researchers consider memory and compare accounts with other evidence.',2,null,'historical-evidence'],
['Why are First Nations histories part of both Australian and world history?','They are long, continuing human histories with knowledge, cultures and connections to Country','They began only when Europeans arrived|They contain only one shared story|They are unrelated to people living today','Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples have diverse, continuing histories. Learning should respect community knowledge and perspectives.',2,null,'world-connections'],
['How did Pacific navigators travel long ocean distances before modern navigation devices?','Using knowledge of stars, waves, winds and other environmental clues','Using modern satellite phones|Following road signs in the sea|Never observing the environment','Pacific navigation traditions combine detailed knowledge, experience and observation. Practices vary among island communities.',2,null,'world-connections'],
['Why can a museum’s collection of old toys be useful historical evidence?','It can help show how childhood, materials and play have changed','Toys never tell us anything about people|Every child owned every displayed toy|All old toys were made on the same day','Everyday objects can reveal skills, materials, interests and differences in people’s lives.',1,null,'daily-life'],
['Which is the best way to place three historical events in chronological order?','Arrange them from earliest to latest using their dates','Arrange them by the longest name|Put the most exciting event first|Sort them by the colour of a picture','Chronology helps us see what happened before and after an event. It does not by itself prove cause and effect.',1,null,'historical-evidence'],
['What does BCE mean on a historical timeline?','Before Common Era','Before Computers Existed|British Calendar Evening|Beginning of Current Events','BCE is a dating label used for years before CE. For example, 500 BCE is earlier than 100 BCE.',2,null,'historical-evidence'],
['Why is it useful to ask “whose story is missing?” when reading history?','Some sources leave out groups or viewpoints','Every source includes everyone equally|Only famous people have a past|There can be only one useful source','Comparing sources helps include different experiences, including ordinary people and communities whose accounts were overlooked.',3,null,'historical-evidence'],
['A town opens a bridge, and shops grow busier the next year. What should a historian do before calling the bridge the only cause?','Check other changes and compare supporting evidence','Assume events close in time always have one cause|Ignore all records|Decide by the bridge’s colour','Events can have several causes. A sequence in time is not enough to establish the only explanation.',4,null,'historical-evidence'],
['Why might a new invention benefit some communities sooner than others?','Cost, access, infrastructure and local needs can differ','Every invention reaches everyone on the same day|All communities have identical resources|An invention has only one possible effect','The spread and effects of technologies depend on social and economic conditions, not just the invention date.',4,null,'historical-evidence'],
['Why should a history of scientific discoveries acknowledge collaborators?','Research often builds on teamwork and earlier discoveries','Every discovery is made entirely alone|Earlier evidence never matters|Only the person in a photograph contributed','Attributing contributions carefully gives a fuller picture of how knowledge develops.',3,null,'history-people']
